$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The application</h2>
<p>The wife applied under Section 24 CPC to transfer Case No. 431/2024 — the husband&rsquo;s divorce petition under Section 13 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 — from the Family Court, Chittorgarh to Family Court No. 1, Bhilwara. Her counsel pointed to inconvenience and hardship, and to a decisive practical fact: her other proceedings — a maintenance case under Section 125 CrPC, a complaint under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, and a case under Section 498A IPC — are all pending at Bhilwara. Moving the divorce petition there would bring the entire cluster of litigation to one station.</p>

<h2>What the Court ordered</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Notice.</strong> Issued to the sole respondent, returnable within four weeks, with process to be filed in two sets within one week and dasti service permitted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Stay.</strong> Proceedings in Case No. 431/2024 before the Family Court, Chittorgarh stand stayed until the next date of hearing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Condition.</strong> If the applicant fails to take effective steps for service, the stay will be revoked — the interim protection is tied to diligent prosecution.</div>
</div>
<p>Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Maneesh Sharma passed the order on 16 November 2026. Nothing in it decides the merits of the transfer; it preserves the position so that the application is not overtaken by events at Chittorgarh.</p>

<h2>The strategy of consolidation</h2>
<p>Transfer practice in matrimonial matters is ultimately about geography and fairness. A wife litigating four cases in two cities attends hearings at both, bears double travel, and risks inconsistent timelines — the divorce trial racing ahead while her maintenance claim idles. Consolidating connected matters at one station serves more than her convenience: it reduces conflicting findings, lets one set of counsel manage the record, and makes settlement conversations realistic because every thread of the dispute sits before courts in one place.</p>
<p>Husbands&rsquo; counsel, for their part, legitimately test such applications: whether the hardship pleaded is real, whether the wife&rsquo;s cases were themselves filed to manufacture a convenient forum, and whether video-conferencing or exemption applications could address the travel burden short of transfer. These are the arguments the Rajasthan High Court will weigh when the application is heard on merits.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>File the transfer application early — before substantial evidence is recorded in the case sought to be transferred, when the balance of convenience is cleanest.</p>
  <p>Plead specifics: distances, dependants, income, dates of the connected cases and their stage. Vague hardship rarely persuades.</p>
  <p>Seek a stay alongside transfer, and then serve promptly — as this order shows, interim protection is conditional on effective steps.</p>
  <p>Remember that intra-State transfers go to the High Court under Section 24 CPC, while inter-State matrimonial transfers lie to the Supreme Court under Section 25 CPC.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports an interim judicial order for general information. The transfer application remains pending and nothing here comments on its eventual outcome.</div>
